mfmediaengine: events are delivered from a work-queue thread, never under the engine's lock.

Wine calls the application's IMFMediaEngineNotify::EventNotify with engine->cs held: on the caller's thread in
Play, Pause, SetSource, SetCurrentTime, SetVolume, SetMuted and the rate setters, and on the Media Foundation
work-queue thread for the session events, the load handler and the sink events. An application whose callback takes
a lock of its own, while another of its threads holds that lock and calls any engine method, deadlocks: thread A holds
R and calls GetCurrentTime, which waits for engine->cs; the work-queue thread holds engine->cs inside EventNotify and
waits for R. Windows' media engine delivers its events asynchronously and takes no lock of its own around them.
Microsoft Flight Simulator 2024 froze this way at its intro video on the M4 (2026-10-16): its main thread and its
"METhread:bo.mp4" thread in RtlpWaitForCriticalSection on each other's sections, one of them unnamed and
heap-allocated like the engine's, 0% CPU, a black screen for good. tests/mfmediaengine/mfdeadlock.c reproduces the
shape with an audio-only engine: DEADLOCK on the engine as shipped, OK with this patch.

Events now go through media_engine_notify(), which appends them to a FIFO under a small lock of their own (never
held during a callback) and schedules one dispatcher work item on the multithreaded queue when none is running; the
dispatcher drains the FIFO in order on its thread and releases the engine reference it holds. Shutdown drains the
FIFO after the session's shutdown, outside engine->cs (a no-op when called from the dispatcher's own thread), and
events raised after Shutdown are dropped. The order of events is unchanged; the thread they arrive on is the work
queue's for every event, as on Windows. Same code in upstream master (not submitted).

